# Abstract

The use of nanoscale metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) as drug delivery vehicles has attracted considerable attention in tumor therapy. In this study, novel biocompatible MOF-based nanocarriers were used as part of a facile and reproducible strategy for precision cancer theranostics. Both diagnostic \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+})\) and therapeutic compounds (doxorubicin, DOX) were incorporated into the multifunctional MOF-based nanocarriers, which exhibited high colloidal stability and promoted \({\sf T}_{1}\) -weighted proton relaxivity and low-pH-activated drug release. The obtained MOF-based nanocarriers exhibited significantly high cellular uptake and efficient intracellular drug delivery into cancer cells, which resulted in high apoptosis and cytotoxicity, in addition to effectively inhibiting the migration of 4T1 breast cancer cells. Moreover, the MOF-based nanocarriers could intensively deliver diagnostic and therapeutic agents to tumors to enable precise visualization of the nanocarrier accumulation and accurate tumor positioning, diagnosis, and imaging-guided therapy using magnetic resonance imaging (MRI). In addition, the functional MOF-based nanocarriers exhibited effective ablation of the primary breast cancer, as well as significant inhibition of lung metastasis with a high survival rate. Therefore, the developed nanocarriers represent a viable platform for cancer theranostics.

Keywords: metal-organic frameworks, drug delivery system, diagnosis, tumor therapy, lung metastasis
# 1. Introduction

The engineering of nanoscale drug delivery systems (i.e., nanocarriers) has been demonstrated to have great potential in cancer treatment[1–3] compared to conventional anticancer agents, which have had low therapeutic outcomes, are sensitive to enzymatic degradation, and have undesirable adverse effect. Nanocarriers can be used to deliver hydrophobic drugs (e.g., paclitaxel) or enzyme-sensitive bioactive compounds (e.g., antibodies and nucleic acids), reduce potential toxicity, extend the half-life of a drug in blood circulation, enhance drug concentrations in tumors/cancer cells, and improve the diagnostic/therapeutic performance[4, 5]. In this regard, numerous nanocarriers have been exploited to conquer the biological barriers related to circulation, the tumor microenvironment, and cancer cells, and carry bioactive compounds into tumors or cancer cells[6]. Nanocarriers with long circulation times and stable profiles are believed to extravasate from leaky tumor blood vessels and accumulate in tumors[7]. In recent years, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed of metal ions or cluster nodes and organic linkers are emerging as a new category of functional materials for drug delivery in oncology[8–13]. MOFs have attracted great attention for drug delivery because of their unique features, which include a large surface area, a three-dimensional (3D) and mesoporous structure, the potential for functionalization, and excellent thermal and chemical stabilities[14–17]. Several MOF-based nanocarriers have been investigated, showing their great potential for drug/gene delivery to tumors[8, 18, 19]. In 2006, Férey et al. first proposed the possibility of using Cr-based MOFs for the delivery of ibuprofen (IBU)[20]. Because of the toxicity of Cr compounds, some less toxic MOFs have been developed to deliver anticancer drugs to tumors for chemotherapy,
such as MIL-100 (Fe)[21]. In addition, MOFs can also be used to deliver other kinds of bioactive compounds, e.g., photosensitizers for effective tumor photodynamic therapy (PDT)[22]. Moreover, nafnium (Hf)-based MOFs have been engineered for both chemotherapy and PDT of tumors[23]. In addition, MOF-based nanocarriers were developed for tumor immunotherapy and gene therapy[24, 25]. Nevertheless, several challenges need to be addressed before this approach can be successfully used for tumor therapy. For instance, nanoscale (e.g., 100 nm) and biocompatible MOFs are needed, and MOF-based drug delivery systems need to be functionalized for effective cancer therapy. Therefore, there is a need to develop innovative MOF-based nanocarriers and verify their performance for tumor precision therapy.

This study examined novel 3D, mesoporous, and biocompatible MOF-based nanocarriers with dual functions for magnetic resonance imaging (MRI)-guided chemotherapy of breast cancer and prohibiting lung metastasis (Figure 1). Nanoscale MOFs containing free carboxylic acid groups (UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)\(_2\)) were synthesized. Paramagnetic metal ions \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+})\) and the anticancer drug DOX were encapsulated inside the porous MOFs \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\&\mathsf{D O}\mathsf{X}\ @\mathsf{M O F})\), while pegylation was included to increase the stability and avoid potential uptake by the reticuloendothelial systems (RES). Integrating both diagnostic and therapeutic functions into one drug delivery system made it possible to envision applications to precision therapy, with the \(\mathsf{T}_{1}\)-weighted contrast agent \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+})\) facilitating cancer diagnosis using MRI, and the incorporated DOX eradicating 4T1 breast tumors. Because metastasis is a major cause of the high mortality rate of cancer patients, the MOF-based nanocarriers were further used to inhibit the lung metastasis of 4T1 cancer and improve the survival rate.
