2. Materials and methods

2.1. Reagents, cell lines, and animals: Zirconium tetrachloride \((\mathsf{Z r C l}_{4} \geq 98\%)\) and 1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylic acid \((\mathsf{H}_{4}\mathsf{B T e C} \geq 98\%)\) were purchased from Wako Pure Chemical Industries (Osaka, Japan). Doxorubicin hydrochloride \((\mathsf{D O X}-\mathsf{H C l} \geq 98\%)\) and manganese (II) chloride \((\mathsf{M n C l}_{2} \geq 98\%)\) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich (St. Louis, USA). D-luciferin potassium \((\geq 98\%)\) was purchased from Dalian Meilun Biological Technology Co., Ltd. (Dalian, China). Fetal bovine serum (FBS) was purchased from Thermo-Fisher Scientific (Waltham, USA). Roswell Park Memorial Institute (RPMI) 1640, and gadopentetate dimeglumine (Gd-DTPA) were purchased from GE Healthcare Life Sciences (Shanghai, China).

1,2-distearoyl-sn-glycero-3-phosphothanolamine-N-[methoxy (polyethyleneglycol-2000)] ammonium salt \((\mathsf{D S P E-P E G}_{2000} > 95\%)\) was purchased from Peng Sheng Biological Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). A cell counting kit-8 (CCK-8) was purchased from MedChemExpress (Monmouth Junction, NJ). An annexin V-APC/propidium iodide (PI) apoptosis kit was purchased from KeyGen Biotech Co., Ltd. (Chengdu, China). Hoechst 33342 was purchased from Dojindo Laboratories (Kumamoto, Japan). Lyso-Tracker Green was purchased from Thermo Fisher Scientific (Waltham, MA). Highly metastatic murine cell lines of breast cancer cells (4T1), human umbilical vein endothelial cells (HUVEC), and murine breast cancer cell line stably expressing firefly luciferase (4T1-Luc) were procured from the American Type Culture Collection (Virginia, USA). BALB/c mice (female, 6 weeks) were purchased from HFK Bioscience Co., Ltd. (Beijing, China). All the animal experiments were approved by the Institutional Animal Care and Treatment Committee of Sichuan University.
2.2. Synthesis and characterization of UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs: The UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs were synthesized using a hydrothermal synthesis procedure[26]. Briefly, 0.86 g of H4BTeC and 0.46 g of ZrCl4 were dissolved in 10 mL of Milli-Q water and heated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to reflux for 24 h. Then, the products were collected and washed several times with Milli-Q water via centrifugation (1400 rpm, 11 min each time) for purification. Next, the obtained MOFs were dispersed in distilled water (10 g/mL) and heated under reflux for 16 h to activate the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs. Finally, the nanoscale UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs were collected after filtration, washed with acetone, and dried in vacuum to obtain a white powder. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns, ultraviolet (UV) absorption spectrum, and Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) spectrum of the obtained UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs were characterized.

2.3. Preparation and characterization of Mn\(^{2+}\) & DOX@MOF: The MRI contrast agent (i.e., Mn\(^{2+}\)) and anticancer drug (i.e., DOX) were loaded into the nanoscale UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs. Briefly, 50 mg of the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs were dissolved in 50 mL of distilled water under stirring, and were purified via filtering with a 0.45 µm polyether sulfone (PES) membrane (diameter: 25 mm). The composition of the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs was characterized using an X-ray diffraction analysis and Jade 5.0. Then, the MnCl\(_2\) (50 mg), 2.5 mL of DOX (4 mg/mL), and DSPE-PEG\(_{2000}\) (75 mg) were successively added dropwise to the solution containing the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs. The mixture was stirred for 24 h to form nanoscale Mn\(^{2+}\)- and DOX-loaded UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs (i.e.,
\(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF). The \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was dialyzed in Milli-Q water for 12 h, concentrated by centrifugation, and finally filtered by passing through a \(0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) PES membrane. In addition, DOX-loaded UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs (i.e., DOX@MOF) were also prepared following the same procedure as described above except for the addition of the \(\mathsf{M n C l}_{2}\). The size distribution and \(\zeta\)-potential of the nanocarriers were evaluated through a dynamic light scattering (DLS) measurement at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) using a Zetasizer Nano ZS (Malvern, UK). The amount of Mn was measured using an inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) (ICP-OES 5100 SVD, Agilent) after treating the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF samples with \(60\%\) \(\mathsf{H N O}_{3}\) overnight and then diluting with \(1\%\) \(\mathsf{H N O}_{3}\) 1000 times \((\mathsf{n}=3)\); the amount of DOX was measured using UV-vis (UV-2600, Shimadzu, Japan) \((\mathsf{n}=3)\). The XRD patterns of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF; UV absorption and FT-IR spectra of the DOX, DOX@MOF, and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF; and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results for the MOFs and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were evaluated. To evaluate the colloidal stability of the obtained nanocarriers, the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was incubated under physiological conditions (10 mM PBS buffer containing 150 mM NaCl, pH 7.4) at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under continuous shaking. Then, the diameter and polydispersity index (PDI) of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were evaluated via DLS at a defined time point.

2.4. Release profiles of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF: The release profile of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was tested under physiological conditions at different pH values. Briefly, 0.4 mL of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was sealed in a dialysis bag (molecular weight cutoff, 6–8 kDa) and dialyzed against 39.6 mL of PBS buffer (pH 5.0, 6.5, and 7.4) containing \(10\%\) FBS at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)
with continuous shaking, to mimic the physiological conditions. At a defined time, the amount of DOX released was measured using UV-vis (UV-2600, Shimadzu, Japan) by removing 1 mL of the solution from the dialysis bag. Meanwhile, the released \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) was measured using the ICP-OES. The results of triplicate tests were used to calculate the average cumulative release of DOX and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\).

2.5. Relaxivity characterization: The proton relaxation rates \((\mathsf{r}_{1})\) of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) & DOX @ MOF in 10 mM PBS buffer (pH 7.4) and of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{I}_{2}\) in water were measured using a 1 T MRI analyzer (NM42-040H-1, Suzhou Niumag Analytical Instrument Corporation, Suzhou, China). Briefly, the longitudinal relaxation time, \(\mathsf{T}_{1}(\mathsf{s})\), values of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) & DOX @ MOF and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{I}_{2}\) at 0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4, and 0.5 mM based on Mn were measured using an inversion-recovery pulse sequence method. Then, the \(\mathsf{r}_{1}\) relaxivity was calculated using the equation \(\mathsf{r}_{1}=(1/\mathsf{T}_{1}-1/\mathsf{T}_{1(0)})/\mathsf{Mn}\), where [Mn] is the sample concentration (mM), \(1/\mathsf{T}_{1(0)}(\mathsf{s}^{-1})\) is the longitudinal relaxation rate without paramagnetic species (i.e., \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\)), and \(1/\mathsf{T}_{1}(\mathsf{s}^{-1})\) is the longitudinal relaxation rate with \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\)-based contrast agents[27].

2.6. In vitro cytotoxicity evaluation: The nanocarrier-induced apoptosis of 4T1 cancer cells was measured using flow cytometry (FCM). Briefly, the 4T1 cancer cells were seeded in 12-well plates at a concentration of \(1\times10^{5}\) cells/well and maintained for 24 h. Then, the 4T1 cancer cells were exposed to DOX, DOX @ MOF, and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) & DOX @ MOF at a DOX concentration of \(1\,\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathsf{mL}\). After incubation for 24 h, the cells were collected and washed with PBS twice, and then stained with the Apoptosis Kit following the manufacturer's instructions.
Finally, the apoptosis was detected using FCM (Cytoflex, Beckman). The 4T1 cancer cells without the added drugs were also investigated as a control.

In addition, the cell viability values of the 4T1 cancer cells against \(\mathsf{M n C l}_{2}\), \(\mathsf{M n}^{2+}@\mathsf{M O F}\), DOX@MOF, \(\mathsf{M n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX were evaluated. Briefly, the 4T1 cells were maintained in RPMI 1640 medium supplemented with \(10\%\) FBS, seeded in 96-well plates at 4000 cells/well, and incubated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). Then, the cancer cells were exposed to different concentrations of \(\mathsf{M n C l}_{2}\), \(\mathsf{M n}^{2+}@\mathsf{M O F}\), DOX@MOF, \(\mathsf{M n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX and incubated for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). Subsequently, \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of CCK-8 was added to each well and incubated for 2 h, and the cell viability was measured using a microplate reader (Cyration 3, Biotek, Vermont, USA) at a wavelength of 450 nm. The cell viability was calculated using the following equation:

Cell viability%=(A sample\(^2\)A 0/A cell\(^2\)A 0) ×100%

where A sample represents to the average absorbance of the cells exposed to the samples, A cell is the average absorbance of the cells without the addition of the samples, and A 0 represents the background absorbance of the RPMI 1640 medium supplemented with \(10\%\) FBS. In addition, the cell viability of the UIO-66(Zr)- \((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs was also evaluated on 4T1 cancer cells and HUVEC normal cells to evaluate the cytotoxicity at different doses.

2.7. In vitro wound healing assay: A wound healing assay was conducted to evaluate the nanocarriers' ability to prohibit the immigration of 4T1 cancer cells. Briefly, the 4T1 cancer cells were seeded in 6-well plates at a concentration of \(5\times10^{5}\) cells per well and incubated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\). Then, the monolayer cells were scratched with a sterile pipette tip in a direction
perpendicular to the line, followed by washing three times with PBS to remove the floating cells. Thereafter, fresh medium samples with \(10\%\) FBS containing UiO-66(Zr)-\((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs, DOX, DOX@MOF, and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF at a DOX concentration of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) were added to different wells and incubated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Each well was photographed using a microscope (Leica, Germany) before and \(24\,\mathsf{h}\) after adding the drugs.

2.8. In vitro cellular uptake evaluation: The cellular uptake values of 4T1 cancer cells for the DOX, DOX@MOF, and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were evaluated using FCM. In brief, the 4T1 cancer cells were seeded in 24-well plates at a concentration of \(5\,\times\,10^{4}\) cell/well and incubated for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . Then, the medium in each well was replaced with fresh medium containing DOX, DOX@MOF, or \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF at a DOX concentration of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) . After exposure to the drug for \(24\,\mathrm{~h~}\) , the cancer cells were washed with PBS three times, harvested, and analyzed using FCM (ACEA, NovoCyte, USA).

Moreover, confocal laser scanning microscopy (CLSM) was used to evaluate the cellular uptake of the nanocarriers. Briefly, the 4T1 cancer cells were seeded in 3.5 cm plates at a concentration of \(2\times10^{4}\) cell/well and incubated for \(24~\mathrm{h}\) . Then, the medium was replaced with fresh medium containing DOX, DOX@MOF, or \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF at a DOX concentration of \(1\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL}\) . After incubation for 1, 6, and \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) , the cells were washed with PBS; the endosomes/lysosomes were stained with LysoTracker Green; and the nuclei were stained with Hoechst 33342. After being washed three times with PBS, the cells were observed using a CLSM excited at 633, 488, and 405 nm, respectively. The yellow color corresponds to the DOX (red) trapped inside the endosomes/lysosomes (green), while the red color corresponds
to the DOX released inside the cytoplasm (Figure 4c).

2.9. Blood circulation and biodistribution study: DOX and DOX@MOF were intravenously injected into subcutaneous 4T1 tumor-bearing mice (BALB/c, female, 6 weeks) at a dosage of 10 mg/kg based on DOX. At 1, 4, and 8 h after injection, the mice were sacrificed, and heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney, and tumor tissues were collected and investigated using an IVIS Lumina Series III (PerkinElmer, USA) in vivo imaging system (IVIS). The average fluorescence intensities of the obtained organs and tumor tissues were quantitatively assessed. Meanwhile, blood was collected from the orbit at defined time points (10 min and 1, 4, 8, and 24 h) after the intravenous injection of the drugs, and the fluorescence intensity was measured using the IVIS. The fluorescence intensity of the DOX in the serum was measured using a multi-mode plate reader (Cytation 5, Bio Tek Instruments, Inc., USA). The fluorescence intensity of the administrated samples was also measured as a control, and the injected dose (%) was calculated using the following equation:

Injected dose % = (fluorescence intensity of collected sample/fluorescence intensity of injected sample) × 100%

2.10. MRI of tumors: Subcutaneous 4T1 tumor-bearing BALB/c mice were scanned using a 7T MRI machine for \( \mathsf{T}_{1} \)-weighted images of a tumor before and after the intravenous injection of \( \mathsf{Mn}^{2+} \) & DOX@MOF at a dosage of 0.127 mmol/kg based on Mn, until the tumor volume reached 100 mm³. Meanwhile, the clinically used contrast agent Gd-DTPA was tested in the same manner for the MRI of cancer with an equal dosage of 0.127 mmol/kg based on Gd. For
the aforementioned \( T_{1} \)-weighted MRI, the spin-echo method was used with the following parameters: \( TE = 9.6 \, \text{ms} \), \( TR = 600 \, \text{ms} \), number of acquisitions (NEX) = 4, slice thickness = 0.8 mm, \( FOV = 40 \times 40 \, \text{mm}^2 \), and matrix size = 256 × 256.

2.11. In vivo antitumor efficacy tested on subcutaneous tumor models: The BALB/c mice (female, 6 weeks) were subcutaneously injected in the abdomen with \( 1 \times 10^6 \) 4T1 breast cancer cells in 100 µL of medium. When the tumor volume reached 40–50 mm\(^3\), the mice were divided into 4 groups (n = 5) with similar average tumor volumes. DOX, DOX@MOF, and \( \text{Mn}^{2+} \& \text{DOX} @ \text{MOF} \) were intravenously injected into the mice in each group through the tail vein at a dosage of 10 mg/kg based on DOX on days 0, 2, 4, and 6, while one group of mice was administrated PBS as a control. The body weight and tumor size were measured every other day, and the tumor volume (V) was calculated using the following equation:

\[
\mathsf{V}=(\mathsf{axb}^2)/2
\]

where (a) and (b) are the major and minor axes of the tumor measured by a caliper. After the experiments, all the mice were sacrificed, and heart, liver, spleen, lung, kidney, and tumor tissues were resected and collected for pathological investigation via hematoxylin and eosin (H&E) staining.

2.12. In vivo antitumor efficacy tested on breast cancer lung metastasis models: The BALB/c mice (female, 6 weeks) were intravenously injected with \( 2.5 \times 10^5 \) 4T1-Luc cancer cells through the tail vein to establish lung metastasis of breast cancer. The lung metastasis of 4T1-Luc breast tumors was confirmed by measuring the luminescence intensity of the cancer
cells with IVIS (IVIS Lumina Series III, PerkinElmer, USA) after the injection of D-luciferin potassium[28]. Then, 6 days after the injection of the 4T1-Luc breast cancer cells, the mice were randomly divided into 3 groups (n = 6 or 7) according to the bioluminescence intensity of metastatic niches in the lung. This day (6 days after the injection of cancer cells) was marked as day 0. DOX and DOX @ MOF were intravenously injected through the tail vein at a dosage of 10 mg/kg based on DOX on days 0, 2, 4, and 6, while one group of mice was intravenously injected with PBS as a control. The bioluminescence intensity and body weight of the mice were monitored every other day, and the survival rate was also recorded. After the experiments, the mice were sacrificed, and the lungs were collected to confirm the metastatic tumors via H&E staining and optical imaging.

# 3. Results and discussion

3.1. Preparation and characterization of MOF-based nanocarriers

The water soluble UiO-66(Zr)-\((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs with free carboxylic acid groups were prepared with zirconium (IV) and H4BTeC using a hydrothermal synthesis procedure and activated by reflux, demonstrating high stability in aqueous solutions[29, 30]. The structure of UiO-66-(COOH)2 consisted of six Zr clusters \([Z r_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}]\) , which were linked to each other by H4BTeC ligands to form a 3D framework of tetrahedron and octahedron cages. The obtained 3D UiO-66(Zr)-\((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs were characterized using XRD, exhibiting the typical peaks in the XRD pattern and validating the successful preparation of UiO-66(Zr)-\((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs (Figure S1)[31–33]. Meanwhile, the FT-IR spectra at 1715 and 3300 cm-1 also confirmed that carboxylic acid groups (COOH) existed in the
UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)\(_2\) MOFs (Figure S2). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) showed that the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)\(_2\) MOFs were nanoscale and monodisperse (Figure 2a and Figure S8a). Their hydrodynamic diameter was approximately 100 nm, with a polydispersity index (PDI) of approximately 0.19, as measured using DLS (Figure 2c).

