Figure 2. Characterization of MOF-based nanocarriers. (a,b) The morphology of UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)\(_2\) MOFs (a) and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF (b) investigated by TEM. Scale bar is 200 nm. (c) The average diameter of MOFs, DOX@MOF and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF. (d) The stability of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF incubated in physiological conditions at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) tested by DLS. (e) The cumulative release profiles of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF at physiological conditions with different pH. (f) The \(\mathsf{r}_{1}\) relaxivity of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF nanocarriers. Data are means ± s.d. \((\mathsf{n}=4)\).
Subsequently, both the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) contrast agent and DOX chemotherapy drug were incorporated inside the porous framework of the UiO-66(Zr)- \((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs (i.e., \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF), because they could be trapped inside the pores or open cavities of the MOFs. Meanwhile, the metal ions (i.e., \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) ) and zirconium (IV) metal centers also contributed a strong synergistic interaction with the carboxylic acid groups and hydroxyl groups in the DOX[34, 35], showing the excellent ability to capture and release cargos. The stable coordination bonds formed between the deprotonated hydroxyl groups in the DOX and numerous Zr sites in the UiO-66(Zr)- \((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs were confirmed by the UV absorption spectrum and XPS spectrum of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF in a previous report[36]. The DOX showed a slight redshift in the UV spectrum (Figures S3 and S4), while the binding energy of the Zr 3d in the XPS spectrum moved to a lower level after DOX loading (Figure S5). The coordination of the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) and carboxylic groups was investigated using FT-IR, and it was found that the stretching vibration peak of \(-\mathsf{C}\!=\!0\) in the carboxyl groups at \(1715~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) was weaker in \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF than in the UiO-66(Zr)- \((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs (Figure S6)[37]. Meanwhile, the disappearance of the DOX peaks at 970 and \(912\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) indicated the interaction between the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) and DOX, which was also reported in a previous study[38]. Notably, there was no change in the structure of the MOFs, which was confirmed by the XRD results for the UiO-66(Zr)- \((\mathsf{C O O H})_{2}\) MOFs and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF (Figures S1 and S7). The surface of the nanocarriers was further wrapped with DSPE-PEG2000 to improve their stability and dispersibility and to extend their half-life in blood circulation. Both the DOX@MOF and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were monodisperse, with hydrodynamic diameters of approximately 113 and 121 nm, respectively, as confirmed using DLS (Figures 2b and c); in
addition, TEM showed that the core size of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was 103 nm (Figure S8b). The \(\zeta\)-potential values of the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)\(_2\) MOFs, DOX@MOF, and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were determined to be -4.9, -12, and -10.4 mV, respectively (Figure S9). The \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF demonstrated high colloidal stability under physiological conditions when incubated in PBS buffer (pH 7.4) at \(37\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), because the diameter and PDI of the nanocarriers showed almost no change within 72 h (Figure 2d), or even after storage for 14 days (Figure S10).

The amount of DOX inside the prepared MOF-based nanocarriers was evaluated in terms of their UV spectra and fluorescence intensity. As illustrated in Figures S4 and S11, the typical UV absorption peaks and fluorescence emission spectra of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF were essentially consistent with those of DOX. In addition, the large amount of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) loading (6.35 mM) in the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was also confirmed by ICP-OES. Therefore, the above tests inferred the successful incorporation of both the anticancer drug (DOX) and MRI contrast agent \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+})\) inside the MOF-based nanocarriers for drug delivery. Moreover, the release of DOX from the \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF nanocarriers was investigated under physiological conditions with different pH values (i.e., pH 5.0, 6.5, and 7.4). It was found that more DOX was released from the MOF-based nanocarriers at a lower pH; approximately \(65\%\), \(50\%\), and \(29.3\%\) of the DOX were released at pH 5.0, 6.5, and 7.4, respectively (Figure 2e). Meanwhile, a similar release profile for \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) from \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was also obtained (Figure S12). The pseudo-first order kinetics, pseudo-second order kinetics, and Higuchi models were used to simulate the release of DOX, which showed that the obtained release data fit the pseudo-second kinetic model (Figure S13).
The cleavage of the chelating between the carboxylic groups and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\), as well as the coordination bonds between the protonated hydroxyl groups in the DOX and the cationic ions, under acidic conditions would accelerate the release of DOX and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\). Because the microenvironment of a tumor (i.e., pH 6.5–6.8) and intracellular compartments (e.g., endosome and lysosome) of cancer cells (i.e., pH 4–6) are acidic, the low pH-triggered release of cargos would facilitate tumor-directed drug delivery and release. Moreover, the tendency of the endogenous phosphate salts in the endosomes to coordinate with the metal ions (i.e., \(Z\mathsf{r}^{4+}\)) also facilitated the release of cargos (i.e., DOX and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\)) from the MOF-based nanocarriers[11, 39]. In addition, the longitudinal relaxivity coefficient \((r_{1})\) of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was measured to be \(18.778\,\mathsf{m M}^{-1}\,\mathsf{s}^{-1}\), which was approximately 3.9 times that of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}\mathsf{C}\mathsf{I}_{2}\) (Figure 2f), demonstrating the high potential for the MRI of tumors.

# 3.2. In vitro cytotoxicity evaluation and wound-healing assay

To evaluate the potential antitumor effect, cytotoxicity, apoptosis, and wound-healing assays of DOX, \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX@MOF were systematically conducted on 4T1 breast cancer cells. First, flow cytometry was used to detect the drug-induced apoptosis of 4T1 breast cancer cells 24 h after exposure to DOX, \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX@MOF (Figure 3a). The apoptosis rates, including both early and late apoptosis, induced by \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF were \(62.76\%\) and \(44.18\%\) (Figure 3b), respectively, which were considerably higher than that of DOX (i.e., \(15.95\%\)); this showed the strong ability of the MOF-based nanocarriers to induce the apoptosis of 4T1 cancer cells. The apoptosis of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF was slightly higher than that of DOX@MOF, because the
\(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) could affect the \(\mathsf{C a}^{2+}\) channel of cancer cells to induce additional apoptosis[40]. In addition, the in vitro cell viabilities with DOX, \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX@MOF were tested on 4T1 breast cancer cells. As shown in Figure 3c, the DOX, \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX@MOF all exhibited significant dose-dependent cytotoxicity (Figure 3c). The half maximal inhibitory concentrations \(\left(\mathsf{I C}_{50}\right)\) of DOX@MOF and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF were 0.38 and \(0.3\,\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathsf{m L}\), respectively, which were slightly higher than that of DOX (i.e., \(0.2\,\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathsf{m L}\)). The \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\)-incorporated MOFs \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\ @\mathsf{M}\mathsf{O F})\) exhibited a cytotoxicity similar to that of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) when tested in 4T1 cells (Figure S14). Meanwhile, the cytotoxicity of the UiO-66(Zr)-(COOH)2 MOFs was also evaluated on both 4T1 cancer cells and HUVEC normal cells, and no evident cytotoxicity was found on either cell line at the tested dose (Figure S15 and 16); this indicated the safety of the MOF-based nanocarriers. In addition, a wound healing assay was utilized to study the ability of the MOF-based nanocarriers to prohibit the migration of 4T1 breast cancer cells, because the migration of cancer cells leads to metastasis in distant organs (e.g., the liver and lungs). A microscopy investigation (Figures 3d and e) showed that the migration of cancer cells was effectively prohibited by both \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF, only showing short migration distances in the range of 84–90 \(\upmu\mathrm{m}\). However, the cancer cells without the treatment drugs (Figures 3d and e) or only treated with MOFs (Figure S17) grew very quickly and migrated a large distance \((>\!400\,\upmu\mathrm{m})\). The migration distance for cancer cells treated with DOX was also as great as \(250~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\) (Figures 3d and e). Therefore, these results indicated that the MOF-based nanocarriers could promote the anticancer efficacy of DOX, as well as inhibit the spreading/migration of cancer cells, facilitating future applications in treating primary and metastatic tumors.
