3.3. Cellular uptake and intracellular drug delivery

The cellular uptake of nanocarriers and intracellular delivery of DOX were confirmed by the flow cytometry and CLSM results. The cellular uptake rates of DOX, \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF, and DOX@MOF by 4T1 cancer cells were analyzed via flow cytometry at 24 h. As observed in the flow cytometry results, the cellular uptake rates of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF were significantly higher than that of DOX (Figures 4a and b), confirming the excellent internalization between the MOF-based nanocarriers and cancer cells. CLSM (Figure 4c) showed that the cellular uptake rates of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF rapidly increased when the incubation time increased from 1 h to 6 h and then 24 h. However, the cellular uptake of DOX was weak until 24 h. According to the CLSM results, remarkably higher levels of DOX were delivered into the 4T1 cancer cells by both \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF and DOX@MOF compared with that of DOX; this was also in accordance with the results obtained using FCM. Moreover, the endosome escape was determined by quantifying the colocalization ratio of DOX and late-endosome/lysosome, as illustrated in Figure 4d. Accordingly, the colocalization ratios of \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) &DOX@MOF (i.e., 0.25) and DOX@MOF (i.e., 0.37) were much lower than that of DOX (i.e., 0.75), because most of the DOX was delivered into the cytoplasm of the cancer cells by the MOF-based nanocarriers through escaping the endosomes/lysosomes. The cationic ions (e.g., \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\)) would facilitate the endosome/lysosome escape of the MOF-based nanocarriers, because the released \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}\) increased the osmotic pressure and unbalanced the cationic ions inside endosomes. In contrast, the majority of the DOX was trapped inside endosomes/lysosomes in cancer cells treated with free DOX. Because DOX acts on topoisomerase II and DNA, the intracellular delivery of DOX is crucially important to induce apoptosis and therapeutic effectiveness[41]. In this
study, the MOF-based nanocarriers markedly promoted the intracellular delivery of DOX by overcoming the cellular barriers, offering improved potency for effective cancer treatment.

