3.4. Blood circulation and biodistribution study

The pharmacokinetic profiles and biodistribution of DOX and the MOF-based nanocarriers were investigated by analyzing the DOX levels in the blood, tumors, and organs after intravenous drug injections into the tail veins of mice with 4T1 subcutaneous tumors. To monitor the biodistribution of the administrated drugs, the tumors and organs, including the heart, liver, spleen, lung, and kidney, were excised at defined time points (1, 4, and 8 h) after the systemic administration, and then checked by IVIS. As shown in ex vivo images (Figures 5a and b), the DOX@MOF levels were prominently increased in the 4T1 breast tumors from 1 to 8 h, whereas the fluorescence intensity of free DOX was barely increased. Notably, the accumulation of DOX@MOF in tumors was obviously higher than that of DOX, indicating the successful drug delivery to tumors by the nanocarriers. With regard to the accumulation in organs, there was no obvious difference in most of the organs, except the kidney and liver, and the distribution of nanocarriers in the other organs was low (Figure S18). The DOX@MOF gradually accumulated in the liver from 1 to 4 h, and then slightly decreased (Figure 5a and Figure S8), which may have been due to the excretion of nanocarriers from the kidney. Meanwhile, only high levels of DOX@MOF were found in the liver at 8 h, which could be interpreted as a result of the gradual uptake of nanocarriers by the RES, such as by macrophages. The retention rates of DOX@MOF and DOX in blood circulation were also monitored. As shown in Figure 5c, the free DOX was rapidly eliminated from the blood circulation, while high levels of DOX@MOF remained. For example, over 18% remained at 8 h, demonstrating the extended half-life in the blood circulation. The long period of circulation of the MOF-based nanocarriers could be ascribed to their colloidal stability and the good
surface modification of pegylation, which could lead to a high accumulation in tumors. The long period of circulation and high tumor accumulation results provided strong evidence suggesting that the MOF-based nanocarriers could improve the pharmacokinetic performance of DOX and effectively deliver drugs to tumors, and could thus facilitate effective tumor chemotherapy.

