3.5. In vivo MRI of tumors for imaging-guided effective tumor ablation

The MOF-based nanocarriers were further used in the MRI of tumors, which is important for cancer diagnosis and molecular imaging-guided therapy. In the \(\mathsf{T}_{1}\)-weighted MRI, a rapid contrast enhancement of the tumor regions was achieved within 1 h with a significant tumor-to-normal (T/N) contrast ratio greater than \(179\%\) \((\mathsf{p}<0.001)\) after the intravenous injection of \(\mathsf{Mn}^{2+}\) & DOX@MOF. The contrast enhancement was even greater at 2 h, with a T/N contrast ratio of \(218\%\). (Figures 6a and b). The high proton \(\mathsf{r}_{1}\) relaxivity and high accumulation of \(\mathsf{Mn}^{2+}\) & DOX@MOF in tumors would have contributed to the high contrast amplification during the MRI of a tumor. In addition, the \(\mathsf{Mn}^{2+}\) released from the MOF-based nanocarriers could readily interact with environmental proteins (e.g., albumin), which could also trigger the nonlinear promotion of the \(\mathsf{r}_{1}\) relaxivity[42, 43], leading to contrast amplification for tumor imaging[27]. However, the contrast in tumors enhanced by an equal dose of the clinically applied contrast agent Gd-DTPA was too weak to detect tumors because the T/N contrast ratio was only \(110\%\) at 1 h and then rapidly decreased (Figure 6a,b); this was similar to the results of some previous investigations[44]. The low contrast enhancement by Gd-DTPA may have been caused by its fast elimination and the absence of tumor-targeting functions. Therefore, the \(\mathsf{Mn}^{2+}\) & DOX@MOF could selectively probe tumors with high sensitivity, endowing a high potential for tumor detection by MRI, while the imaging results
could provide the precise orientation of a tumor and pathological information about it for diagnosis and imaging-guided therapy.

