3.6. MOF-based nanocarriers for effective inhibition of lung metastatic tumors

The migration of cancer cells from the primary tumors leads to metastasis, which is a major factor in approximately \(90\%\) of the deaths of cancer patients, and is difficult to treat with conventional drugs[46]. Therefore, the MOF-based nanocarriers were further tested to determine whether they would inhibit tumor metastasis. Breast cancer lung metastasis models were generated by the intravenous injection of 4T1-Luc cancer cells[47], because the circulating cancer cells could be trapped in the capillaries of a lung and form a metastasis. The metastasis in the lung could be analyzed by detecting the bioluminescence intensity of the 4T1-Luc cancer cells after injecting D-luciferin potassium. Bioluminescence could be observed in the lungs of mice 6 days after injecting the 4T1-Luc cancer cells, indicating the successful creation of the breast cancer lung metastasis models (Figure 7a). The metastatic tumor growth rate was determined by measuring the bioluminescence intensity of the ex vivo images (Figure 7b). As shown in Figures 7a and b, intensive lung metastasis could be seen in mice after administering the PBS and DOX, and was associated with rapidly increased bioluminescence intensities and the deaths of the mice. However, the lung metastasis was
effectively prohibited by the administration of DOX @ MOF (Figure 7a), which was associated with a considerably lower growth rate than those of the control groups, i.e., PBS and DOX (Figure 7b). More impressively, after the administration DOX @ MOF, only 1 mouse (out of a total of 7) was dead at day 11, while all of mice that had been treated with PBS and DOX were dead by day 12 (Figure 7c). This indicated that the MOF-based nanocarriers could effectively improve the survival rate of mice with breast cancer lung metastasis. Moreover, the bodyweight of the mice did not change significantly (Figure 7d). In addition, the metastasis in the lungs was further confirmed by checking the metastatic niches in the lungs using optical imaging and H&E staining. As seen in Figure 7e, numerous metastatic tumors existed in the lungs of the PBS and DOX groups, while few metastatic niches were found in the lungs of the DOX @ MOF group; this indicated that the DOX @ MOF could effectively prohibit the formation of lung metastasis.
# 4. Conclusion

In conclusion, nanoscale MOF-based nanocarriers incorporating both an MRI contrast agent and anticancer drug were successfully developed for tumor diagnosis and therapy. The multi-functional MOF-based nanocarriers could efficiently delivery an anticancer drug (e.g., DOX) into cancer cells through cationic ions, which benefited from an endosome/lysosome escape pathway. This induced the remarkable apoptosis of 4T1 breast cancer cells and inhibited their migration. The \(\mathsf{Mn}^{2+}\) & DOX @ MOF could effectively deliver diagnostic and therapeutic compounds to tumors for precision therapy because the tumors could be sensitively and selectively probed for accurate detection by MRI. In addition, the MOF-based nanocarriers could remarkably suppress the growth of the primary 4T1 breast tumors and inhibit the lung metastasis to significantly increase the survival rate.

These multi-functional MOF-based nanocarriers could efficiently deliver drugs to tumors to significantly improve the therapeutic outcomes of the conventional anticancer drugs, which would improve patient care in future clinical applications. In addition, encapsulating imaging probes within the same MOF-based nanocarriers would provide similar pharmacokinetics and tumor accumulation profiles as anticancer drugs. This would facilitate the visualization of the accumulation of nanocarriers in tumors[48], provide accurate positioning and anatomical information for tumor treatment[49–51], and make it possible to monitor the therapeutic outcomes[52]. Compared to most MOF-based nanocarriers with only a single function[21,22], e.g., therapy or diagnosis, this study investigated MOF-based nanocarriers for both therapy and diagnosis, which could facilitate precision tumor therapy because molecular imaging is an important approach to optimize the treatment outcomes[53]. Moreover, the effective
